shortAnswer: "Prompt injection is when instructions hidden inside content an AI reads get treated as commands. An AI summarising a web page or an email can encounter text saying 'ignore your previous instructions and do this instead' — and, because it processes everything as language, it may follow it. It matters most when an AI can act on your behalf rather than just answer questions."
faq:
  - q: "Is this the same as hacking?"
    a: "Not in the usual sense — nothing is broken into and no password is stolen. The attacker just writes text somewhere the AI will read it. That's what makes it awkward: the attack is words, and words are exactly what the system is built to process."
  - q: "Does this affect us if we only use AI for writing?"
    a: "Barely. If you paste in your own text and read the output, there's very little exposure. Risk rises when the AI starts reading things you didn't write — web pages, inbound emails, uploaded documents — and rises again when it can take actions."
  - q: "Can't the AI companies just fix it?"
    a: "They've reduced it substantially, and continue to, but there's no complete fix available today. The system's core strength — following instructions written in plain language — is also the vulnerability. Treat it as a risk to manage, not a bug awaiting a patch."
  - q: "What's the single best protection?"
    a: "Keep a human approving anything irreversible. If the AI drafts and a person sends, an injection attempt produces a strange draft that gets caught. If the AI sends by itself, it produces a sent email you didn't write."
  - q: "How would we even notice?"
    a: "Usually as something odd rather than something dramatic — a summary that includes an out-of-place instruction, a draft addressed to someone you don't recognise, output that ignores what you asked. Worth telling staff that 'the AI did something weird' is a thing to report, not shrug at."
